WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:10.820
okay let's do some practice regarding
enthalpy of combustion so the question

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:17.550
asks how much heat is released upon the
combustion of 1.00 litres of isooctane

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:24.689
and that has the formula C8H18. now in
order to answer this question we will

00:00:24.689 --> 00:00:29.519
need to know that the density of
isooctane is zero point six nine two

00:00:29.519 --> 00:00:34.710
grams per milliliter and we also need to
know that the Delta H of combustion for

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:41.969
iso-octane is negative 5461 kilojoules
per mole so this is all the data you

00:00:41.969 --> 00:00:43.920
need go ahead and give this a try.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:06.900
so let's start out with our one point
zero zero liters of isooctane what are

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:10.799
we gonna do first well we know we're
gonna do some kind of stoichiometry so

00:01:10.799 --> 00:01:14.610
we're gonna want to try to get two moles
so how do we do this well we've got

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:18.900
liters and our density was in grams per
milliliter so we're gonna need in

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:23.820
milliliters so let's just convert this
into milliliters first we've got one

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:28.560
point zero zero liters times a thousand
and since we can only have three sig

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:35.070
figs that is going to be one point zero
zero times 10 to the 3 milliliters. now

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:40.619
as we said we have that density so we
can convert to grams one point zero zero

00:01:40.619 --> 00:01:46.759
times 10 to the 3 milliliters times zero
point six nine two grams per milliliter

00:01:46.759 --> 00:01:55.470
milliliters will cancel and we'll get
692 grams so now that we have a mass of

00:01:55.470 --> 00:02:01.110
isooctane we want to use that mass to
get moles because that way we can use

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:07.920
the Delta H of combustion that we were
given so 692 grams times 1 mol / 114

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:11.520
grams that's what we get just by adding
up all the carbon and hydrogen atoms

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:17.050
that is going to give us six
point zero seven moles so we have six

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:23.020
point zero seven moles of isooctane in
that one liter of isooctane and then

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:27.940
lastly we can use our thermochemical
data we can do six point zero seven

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:34.180
moles times negative five thousand 461
kilojoules that are released for every

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:40.060
mole of iso-octane and we get negative
three point three one times ten to the

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:48.940
four kilojoules that is how much energy is associated with the combustion of one liter of isooctane.

